  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,y)}=(-1)^{3}+(-1)=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=(0)^{2}+0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=(1)^{3}+1=2,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is equal to $2$ for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/45427ef9ff00752ad528335ae51143c27fcc01d9c535d2fe380edfbbfdb9b167.png)",
